          %h2#Differentiation
            %strong Differentiation
          %p
            We introduce the standard concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#DifferentialFormsLayerMod"} Model Layer
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functionals"} local action functionals
          %p
            We define differential forms on general smooth spaces seamlessly in terms of the smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ∈
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Smooth
                  %mn 0
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\in \in Smooth0Type
            of differential forms. This has the special property that it is, for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
            %em
              non-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+object"} concrete
            smooth space. In
            %em
              %a{:href => "#DifferentialFormsLayerSem"} Semantic Layer
            below we take this as occasion to discuss the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+objects"} concrete objects
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+topos"} local topos
            %em concretification
            of the smooth mapping space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,\Omega^n]
            for any smooth space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                smooth (moduli) space of differential forms on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em
              %a{:href => "#ActionFunctionalsForChernSimonsTypeGaugeTheories"} Action functionals for Chern-Simons type gauge theories
            the theory of
            %em concretification
            in a local topos is a central ingredient in the
            %em canonical existence
            of certain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
          %p
            The process of concretification involves the general abstract notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} images
            %a{:href => "#DifferentialFormsLayerSyn"} Syntactic Layer
            here.
          %h3#DifferentialFormsLayerMod Model Layer
          %p
            We have seen above in
            %em
              %a{:href => "TheContinuumRealWorldLine"} The continuum real (world-)line
            that that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is the basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinematics"} kinematical structure
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+spaces"} path spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathbb{R}, X]
            from example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SmoothPathSpace"}
            are to be thought of as the smooth spaces of
            %em trajectories
            (for instance of some
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to X
          %p
            But moreover,
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is encoded by
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+functionals"} functionals
                on such trajectories
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S \colon \left[I, X\right] \to \mathbb{R}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            is the standard unit
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
          %p
            Such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
            we discuss in their own right in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#VariationalCalculus"} Variational calculus
            below. Here we first examine in detail a fundamental property they all have: they are supposed to be
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local
          %p
            Foremost this means that the value associated to a trajectory is
            %em built up incrementally
            from small contributions associated to small sub-trajectories: if a trajectory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            is decomposed as a trajectory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_1
            followed by a trajectory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S(\gamma) = S(\gamma_1) + S(\gamma_2)
                  \\,.
          %p
            As one takes this property to the limit of iterative subdivision, one finds that action functionals are entirely determined by their value on
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
              displacements
            along the worldline. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \colon \mathbb{R} \to X
            denotes a path and “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi γ
                    %mo ˙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dot \gamma(x)
            ” denotes the corresponding “infinitesimal path” at worldline parameter
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi γ
                    %mo ˙
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{d}S(\dot \gamma)_x  \in \mathbb{R}
                  \\,,
          %p
            to be read as “the small change
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d}S
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            along the infinitesimal path
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi γ
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dot \gamma_x
          %p
            This function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d}S
            that assigns numbers to infinitesimal paths is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear+form"} bilinear form
            = succeed "," do
              %em infinitesimal differences
            = succeed "." do
              %em differentials
          %p
            We define smooth differential forms on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#DifferentialFormsOnAbstractCoordinateSystem"} Differential forms on abstract coordinate sysetms
          %p
            Then we discuss how this induces a notion of smooth differential forms on general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#SmoothUniversalModuliSpaceOfDifferentialForms"} Smooth universal moduli space of differential forms
              \.
          %p Further below we provide a precise version of the statement that “Differential 1-forms are differential measures along paths.” in
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#1FormsAsSmoothFunctors"} Differential 1-forms are smooth incremental path measures
              \.
          %h4#DifferentialFormsOnAbstractCoordinateSystem Differential forms on abstract coordinate systems
          %p
            We introduce the basic concept of a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %em
              %a{:href => "#SmoothUniversalModuliSpaceOfDifferentialForms"} Differential forms on smooth spaces
            we use this to define differential forms on any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth space
          #Differential1FormsOnCartesianSpaces.num_defn
